WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.500
Animated virus symbols introduce a pandemic context. Illustrations connect school attendance, decision-making, infection risk and the cost of mitigation. This is a research explainer, not footage of an outbreak.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:49.700
A clipboard and magnifying glass introduce scenario analysis. A U.S. map with school icons appears beside a chart of school levels and months. Bubble size and a labeled color scale encode modeled infection risk. School categories include prekindergarten, elementary, middle, high, secondary and combined schools.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:01:10.000
The map and charts compare school-level infection risk, ventilation needs and annual energy costs. Intervention labels include ventilation increases, MERV filter grades and different proportions of students learning online. The original study and its HTML tables provide the detailed numerical comparisons.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.000
An open book pairs gears for operations with a light bulb and dollar sign for energy costs, illustrating how research can inform school operation.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
A masked teacher stands beside masked students at desks. Stylized virus particles float above them, and an air-handling unit stands near the board. This is an animated classroom, not a monitored or measured school.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:39.000
A fan inside a school-building outline represents ventilation. Four labeled illustrations show exhaled air, student attendance, climate and utility costs. Labels, people, a thermometer and a meter distinguish the factors independently of color.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:52.000
The animation links school operation with infection control and energy use. Icons and an animated balance scale show the relationship between lower infection risk and higher cost.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:17.000
The balance scale and illustrated decision-makers connect the trade-off analysis with school preparedness, response, recovery, health and sustainability. These are research and policy aims, not documented school outcomes.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:26.867
Closing credits: Energy Cost Assessment for Reducing Airborne Infection Risks in U.S. Schools. Contact: Shuai Li. Affiliation: University of Tennessee Knoxville. Sponsor: National Science Foundation. The sponsor logo remains on the source credit strip.
